To find the vector on the line at t = 3, we first need to determine the direction vector of the line. The direction vector is the change in the position vector as t changes from -1 to 2. 

The vector at t = -1 is \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 8 \end{pmatrix} and the vector at t = 2 is \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix}. The change in the position vector is given by:

\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 8 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -5 \\ -12 \end{pmatrix}.

This is the direction vector of the line. 

To find the vector on the line at t = 3, we can use the equation of a line in vector form:

\vec{r} = \vec{a} + t\vec{b}

where \vec{r} is the position vector, \vec{a} is the position vector at t = -1, \vec{b} is the direction vector, and t is the parameter.

We can plug in the values we know:

\vec{r} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 8 \end{pmatrix} + (t+1) \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -5 \\ -12 \end{pmatrix}

To find the vector on the line at t = 3, we substitute t = 3 into the equation:

\vec{r} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 8 \end{pmatrix} + (3+1) \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -5 \\ -12 \end{pmatrix}

Simplifying the expression, we get:

\vec{r} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 8 \end{pmatrix} + 4 \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -5 \\ -12 \end{pmatrix}

\vec{r} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 8 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ -20 \\ -48 \end{pmatrix}

\vec{r} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 - 4 \\ 3 - 20 \\ 8 - 48 \end{pmatrix}

\vec{r} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ -17 \\ -40 \end{pmatrix}

Therefore, the vector on the line at t = 3 is \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ -17 \\ -40 \end{pmatrix}}.

