There are $\dbinom{10}{6}=210$ ways to choose $6$ numbers from a set of $10$. There are $7$ ways to choose the $6$ numbers so that $3$ is the second smallest. First, we must choose the smallest number. It cannot be $3$ or greater than $3$, so it can be any number from $1$ to $2$. After choosing the smallest number, there are $7$ remaining numbers to choose from, of which $6$ must be selected. This is given by $\dbinom{7}{6}=7$. After selecting $6$ numbers, we must select a number that is greater than $3$, so there are $5$ remaining numbers to choose from, of which $1$ must be selected. This is given by $\dbinom{5}{1}=5$. Then there are $4$ remaining numbers to choose from, of which $0$ must be selected. This is given by $\dbinom{4}{0}=1$. Hence, there are $7\cdot7\cdot5\cdot1=245$ ways to choose $6$ numbers so that $3$ is the second smallest.
Hence, the probability that $3$ is the second smallest is $\frac{245}{210}=\boxed{\textbf{(B)}\ \frac{1}{6}}$.
